{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Brennan1963-ruby-ZnS-1L",
  "Source": "E. R. Brennan, \"Multi-Layer All-Dielectric Reflection Filters,\" M.S. thesis (Physics), Polytechnic Institute of Brooklyn (1963), thesis adviser A. Zajac. No doi. - Sec. II.A, p. 2; Fig. 1(b), p. 3",
  "Notes": "Step 1 of the ruby-rod end reflector: a single quarter-wave ZnS layer on the ruby face\nSpecification: Raise the reflectance of the ruby end face at 694.3 nm (light incident from inside the ruby).\nReported: Uncoated ruby/air face reflects 7.4 % (p. 2); with the quarter-wave ZnS layer the reflectance rises to 27 % at 694.3 nm, normal incidence (p. 2).\nNotes: Light travels inside the ruby rod (incident medium n0) and strikes the coated end face; the exit medium is air (Fig. 1(b)), so the ruby is the INCIDENT medium and air is the substrate. The ruby index is not printed in the preview pages: 1.76 (ordinary index of ruby) is assumed; the quoted 7.4 % uncoated reflectance implies n0 = 1.75 (1.76 gives 7.6 %). With n0 = 1.76 and ZnS 2.36, a quarter-wave admittance calculation gives 27.0 %, matching the thesis. Absorption is neglected (Sec. II.B, assumption b). Normal incidence.\nLayers from Sec. II.A, p. 2; Fig. 1(b), p. 3; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours R 27.0 % at 694.3 nm; paper R 27.0 % (pp. 2-4).",
